# Strong Types (mp-units)

All distances and angles use the types from `common/include/Common/Units.h`.

## Required types

`PhysicalLength`, `XLength`, `YLength`, `ZLength`, `Position3D`, `HorizontalAngle`, `AltitudeAngle`,
`Orientation`.

`XLength`/`YLength`/`ZLength` are distinct quantity specs, so mixing axes is a compile error — that is the
point, don't cast around it. Ex3 adds `constexpr operator+` and `operator-` on `Position3D`; use them
instead of writing per-axis arithmetic.

## Rules

```cpp
// BAD - e16: numerical fields instead of strong types
void move(double distance_cm);
struct Waypoint { double x, y, z; };

// GOOD
common::types::MovementResult advance(common::PhysicalLength distance);
common::Position3D waypoint;
```

```cpp
// BAD - e03: unwrapping mp-units for math
double d = length.numerical_value_in(cm);
double x = pos.x.numerical_value_in(cm) + 1.0;

// GOOD - quantity arithmetic
auto next_x = pos.x + 10.0 * cm;
auto dist = si::sqrt(dx * dx + dy * dy);
```

## Boundaries

YAML and `.npy` parsing reads plain numbers — convert to strong types **immediately** at the parse
boundary, so no raw `double` cm/deg value travels further than the parser. Same on the way out: unwrap only
in the YAML/log writer.

Voxel indices are the legitimate exception: they are dimensionless `std::size_t`/`int`, produced inside the
map implementation from `(pos - offset) / resolution`. Keep that conversion in one place
(`docs/map3d-contract.md`), not scattered across the algorithm.

## Aliases

`Units.h` already provides `mp`, `isq`, `si`, `cm`, and `deg` inside `namespace common`. Use those.

**Never** write a per-function alias like `namespace su = mp_units::si::unit_symbols;` — this was a severe
ex1 deduction (e17s). Import once at file scope if you need more symbols.
